            "description": "health_probe_port is the plaintext gRPC port for k8s health probes.\n Kernel's hand-written bootstrap bypasses the generated ServiceManager,\n so this field must be on Kernel.Config (not only on Grpcconfig.Config).\n Default matches the platform-wide health probe port.",
            "default": 8444
          },
          {
            "name": "grants",
            "type": "message",
            "description": "grants seeds the standing delegation Grant catalog at boot, loaded into\n the live grants store the same way delegates (above) are — there is no\n runtime grant-creation RPC yet, so this is the only way a Grant exists\n today.",
            "repeated": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 4096 elements"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 4096,
                "unit": "elements",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "message": {
              "name": "Grant",
              "package": "kernel",
              "description": "Grant is a standing delegation: a user authorizes a delegate (an intermediary\n service, e.g. an agent acting on a schedule) to act on their behalf later,\n asynchronously. Kernel\n stores the GRANT, never a minted token — the delegate holds only `id`.\n\n On use the delegate presents its own S2S identity plus `id`; kernel replays\n `claims` into OPA fresh (the same EntitlementEvalInput shape\n `IdentityServiceImpl.buildClaimsStruct` already builds from a token payload),\n intersects the result with `authorized_scope`, and mints a short-lived token\n (sub=subject, act=delegate_name, azp=kernel — kernel mints Grant tokens\n directly, so azp is always the closed-enum `kernel` value; the delegate name\n goes on the `act` claim instead). Fresh OPA gives \"cannot exceed the user's\n current authority\"; the recorded scope gives \"the user downscoped it\". Nothing\n is snapshotted that can go stale except the claims themselves — which catches\n policy changes, never IDP changes.\n\n This message is carried in KernelDiskState and mutated at runtime by\n Mint.RevokeGrant (revoked_at_ms) only; there is no create/list RPC yet — the\n catalog is still seeded from Config.grants at boot.\n\n PROVISIONAL: this field set is a prerequisite storage layer built ahead of,\n not instead of, the design conversation with the maintainer. What is settled:\n id, delegate_name, subject, authorized_scope, claims, created_at_ms,\n expires_at_ms, revoked_at_ms. NOT settled: how the system learns a user's IDP\n claims changed or the user was deleted — claim-provenance and epoch are still\n open and may add fields here.",
              "fields": [
                {
                  "name": "id",
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "id is the opaque grant identifier the delegate holds and presents. Unique\n across the cluster.",
                  "required": true,
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_required",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "bool",
                      "bool_value": true,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    },
                    {
                      "rule": "default_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 32768,
                      "defaulted": true,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "default_bound"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "delegate_name",
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "delegate_name is the Delegate.name of the intermediary holding this grant.\n It becomes the minted token's act claim (azp on a Grant mint is always\n kernel — see the message comment above).",
                  "required": true,
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 512 bytes"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_required",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "bool",
                      "bool_value": true,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    },
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 512,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "subject",
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "subject is the user this grant acts for — the sub of the token that will be\n minted, and the identity whose claims were recorded below.",
                  "required": true,
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 512 bytes"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_required",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "bool",
                      "bool_value": true,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    },
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 512,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "authorized_scope",
                  "type": "message (recursive reference: entitlements.TokenEntitlements)",
                  "description": "authorized_scope is the per-service scope the user recorded at consent time.\n Every mint intersects the fresh OPA result with this, so the grant can only\n ever narrow the user's live authority, never widen it."
                },
                {
                  "name": "claims",
                  "type": "message",
                  "description": "claims are the user's IDP claims as captured at consent time, in the same\n google.protobuf.Struct shape that userentitlements.EntitlementEvalInput.claims\n takes, so they can be replayed into OPA verbatim."
                },
                {
                  "name": "created_at_ms",
                  "type": "int64",
                  "description": "created_at_ms is the consent time, epoch milliseconds.",
                  "constraints": [
                    "must be \u003e= 0"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_ge",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 0,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "expires_at_ms",
                  "type": "int64",
                  "description": "expires_at_ms is when the grant itself stops being usable, epoch\n milliseconds. 0 means no expiry (revocation only).",
                  "constraints": [
                    "must be \u003e= 0"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_ge",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 0,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "revoked_at_ms",
                  "type": "int64",
                  "description": "revoked_at_ms is when the grant was revoked via Mint.RevokeGrant, epoch\n milliseconds. 0 means not revoked. A revoked grant is MARKED, never\n deleted, so the record remains as an audit trail of the delegation that\n existed and when it was withdrawn. Every mint from the grant and every\n later exchange of a token minted from it is denied once this is set\n (IdentityServiceImpl.assertGrantStillActive), so revocation kills\n already-minted derived tokens within one request.",
                  "constraints": [
                    "must be \u003e= 0"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_ge",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 0,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "trace_id",
                  "type": "string",
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "default_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 32768,
                      "defaulted": true,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "default_bound"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "noop",
                  "type": "bool"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "name": "ratis_listener",
            "type": "message (recursive reference: host.Host)",
            "description": "ratis_listener is the bind address for the embedded Apache Ratis peer\n group that backs the FencedLock service. Kernel is the only service in the\n fleet that runs Ratis. Peers are derived from `members` above (same hosts,\n this port) so lock membership tracks cluster membership and there is no\n second topology to keep in sync. Default port 5002 — the next one after\n hazelcast_listener's 5001."
