          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ],
      "message": {
        "name": "LlmProviderSettings",
        "package": "llmsettings",
        "description": "LlmProviderSettings is ONE person's LLM API settings. It is both the stored row and\n the read response's item type, deliberately: `(spec.store_principal)` and\n `(spec.store_namespace)` filter repeated fields OF THIS TYPE on the way out,\n so a bespoke response row would be a read path with the generated backstops\n switched off (mailbox.ListHeldMailResponse says the same thing).\n\n Returning the stored type is only safe because `api_key` carries\n (spec.redact) — see there.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "owner",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "owner is the collection key AND the row's owning principal.\n\n (spec.indexed) makes it the state collection's key, so there is exactly one\n row per person by construction rather than by a uniqueness check somebody\n has to remember to write. (spec.store_principal) is what holds a caller's\n reads to their own row: the generated wrapper filters every repeated\n LlmProviderSettings field in a response down to rows the caller owns, so the\n handler does not check and there is nothing left for it to get wrong.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "endpoint_url",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "endpoint_url is the LLM API base URL.\n\n HTTPS ONLY, and that is not tidiness: this field's companion is a bearer\n secret, and the service that eventually calls this endpoint would put that\n secret in an Authorization header. Permitting http:// here would let a\n person configure their own key's exfiltration. (spec.assert_url) is the\n generated check — nothing in the handler parses a URL.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 2048 bytes",
              "must be a valid URL (scheme: URL_SCHEME_HTTPS)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 2048,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_url",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "enum",
                "enum_value": "URL_SCHEME_HTTPS",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "api_key",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "api_key is the secret, and (spec.redact) REDACT_MODE_DROP is what makes\n storing it here safe.\n\n DROP is the hard floor: the generated sanitize transform clears this field\n on EVERY egress copy and no runtime entitlement can loosen it, so the value\n is write-only from a caller's point of view. That transform is emitted into\n the generated service wrapper for unary responses, not into this service's\n handler — the handler could not leak the key by forgetting to clear it,\n because it is not the thing doing the clearing.\n\n tacplus also injects a companion `api_key_redacted` bool alongside this\n field and sets it on the sanitized copy. It is NOT the \"is a key saved\"\n signal a form needs: the transform sets it unconditionally, so it is true\n on every response whether or not anything is stored. That question is\n answered by api_key_set below.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 512 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 512,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "data_entitlement_mode": "drop"
          },
          {
            "name": "api_key_set",
            "type": "bool",
            "description": "api_key_set says whether a key is stored, and exists because the redacted\n field cannot say so — a dropped value and an absent one are both the empty\n string on the wire. Maintained by the handler on write.\n\n It is what lets the form render \"a key is saved — replace it?\" rather than\n an empty box that silently means either thing."
          },
          {
            "name": "api_key_hint",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "api_key_hint is the last Config.api_key_hint_chars characters of the stored\n key, so a person with several keys can tell WHICH one is saved. Not\n redacted: it is a deliberate, bounded disclosure, and the bound is the\n config knob rather than this field's own max_size so that lowering the knob\n takes effect on the next write instead of needing a proto change.\n\n 8 is the ceiling Config.api_key_hint_chars enforces; this bound restates it\n so an oversized value is rejected by generated validation even if a future\n handler computes it wrong.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 8 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 8,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "updated_at_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "updated_at_ms is when the row was last written. Server-assigned.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "namespace",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "namespace is the row's owning TENANT, by exact string equality against the\n reader's namespace claim. (spec.store_namespace) generates the read-side\n egress filter on every surface returning this row; the write half is\n UpdateLlmSettingsRequest.namespace's (spec.namespace).\n\n Not a query field: the field exists to BE the egress boundary, and a\n boundary a caller can predicate on is not one.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "api_key_redacted",
            "type": "bool",
            "description": "tacplus data-entitlement: per-field redacted indicators. Set true by\n the generated sanitize transform when the paired field is redacted;\n lets a consumer distinguish a redacted value from a genuine empty one."
          },
          {
            "name": "trace_id",
