{
  "name": "BusinessHours",
  "package": "scotty",
  "description": "BusinessHours is when this person is taken to be working. It is KNOWLEDGE in\n §1's sense — \"what was learned\" — not a setting, and nothing in the API lets\n a caller author it.\n\n TWO LAYERS, and `source` says which one produced the values in this message:\n\n   PRIOR    — an industry+geography default, which is what a brand-new record\n              with zero history has. It is a guess, and it is marked as one.\n   OBSERVED — refined from the person's own recorded activity once there is\n              enough of it to say anything.\n\n Only the PRIOR layer is WRITTEN today. The observed layer is shape here and\n nothing else, deliberately: the naive refinement (widen the window to cover\n every hour a read was seen at) would defeat the thing the deferral exists to\n protect — one 11pm read would make 11pm business hours. That is a question\n about what evidence of \"working\" means, not an implementation detail, and it\n is written up rather than guessed. See\n loopers/schematic/decisions/business-hours-inferred-not-configured.md.\n\n SINGLE CONCEPT. This is the same \"business hours\" feature-ideas.md's loop\n cadence needs (\"looped more frequently Mon-Fri during business hours\"). Do\n not add a second notion of it.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "source",
      "type": "enum",
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_enum_defined",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "bool",
          "bool_value": true,
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "BUSINESS_HOURS_SOURCE_PRIOR",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "PRIOR: an industry+geography default. No activity data went into it."
        },
        {
          "name": "BUSINESS_HOURS_SOURCE_OBSERVED",
          "number": 2,
          "description": "OBSERVED: refined from this person's own recorded activity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "start_of_day_minutes",
      "type": "int32",
      "description": "Minutes past LOCAL midnight, start \u003c end. Nothing validates these on the\n way in because nothing writes them but the service itself; a stored row\n that does not describe a usable window is ignored on the read path in\n favour of the prior, rather than being repaired in place."
    },
    {
      "name": "end_of_day_minutes",
      "type": "int32"
    },
    {
      "name": "working_days",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "The days that are working days. Repeated rather than a mask so the value\n reads as itself in a dump. An empty set is not a usable window, and is\n treated the same way an inverted start/end is — see above.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 7 elements"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 7,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "WEEKDAY_MONDAY",
          "number": 1
        },
        {
          "name": "WEEKDAY_TUESDAY",
          "number": 2
        },
        {
          "name": "WEEKDAY_WEDNESDAY",
          "number": 3
        },
        {
          "name": "WEEKDAY_THURSDAY",
          "number": 4
        },
        {
          "name": "WEEKDAY_FRIDAY",
          "number": 5
        },
        {
          "name": "WEEKDAY_SATURDAY",
          "number": 6
        },
        {
          "name": "WEEKDAY_SUNDAY",
          "number": 7
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "utc_offset_minutes",
      "type": "int32",
      "description": "utc_offset_minutes is the GEOGRAPHY half of the prior, in the crudest form\n that is still correct for the thing it is used for: a fixed offset, no\n DST and no tz database. A Texas crew reading a thread at 23:03 local is\n 23:03 whether or not the offset moved an hour in March, and the label the\n deferral produces is a date-and-time, not an instant anyone schedules\n against. A real tz identifier is future work, noted in the decision doc."
    },
    {
