{
  "name": "ScottyagentState",
  "package": "scottyagent",
  "description": "ScottyagentState is the service's state root, hosted by kernel.\n\n THE INSTANCE REGISTRY IS NOT HERE, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. The plan item\n asks for a \"kernel-state instance registry\"; tacplus already generates one\n for every service — `ServiceHealthState`, kernel-hosted, CRUD+Watch, reached\n through `sm.state_clients().services().service_health()` with\n `get_instances()`, `fetch_all_instances()` and `subscribe_instances()`.\n Declaring a second collection of instance rows here would be a hand-rolled\n duplicate of generated infrastructure, which is the one thing this platform's\n house rules forbid outright. And the job the plan phrase implies for it —\n knowing which instance owns which user — is a job the design record deletes:\n there is no dispatch-exclusivity lock and no ownership to record.\n workers/big8-p1-scotty-agent-result.md §3 shows the generated registry.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "cursors",
      "type": "message",
      "description": "One cursor per user. Bounded like every collection here — tacplus rejects\n an unbounded repeated field at generation. 1,000,000 is the same order\n scotty's own person-scoped collections use.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1000000 elements"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1000000,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "message": {
        "name": "AgentCursor",
        "package": "scottyagent",
        "description": "AgentCursor is one row per user: everything a cycle needs to know about what\n the previous cycle did. It lives in KERNEL STATE, and that placement is a\n deliberate departure from the design record's \"all of Scotty's state is a\n Stratum record\" — written up in workers/big8-p1-scotty-agent-questions.md §1\n rather than made silently. The three reasons, briefly:\n\n   - The design record's own requirement is that a write \"must be visible to\n     the very next cycle even if it runs moments later on a different\n     instance\". A record reaches Stratum through an EventBus publish and an\n     asynchronous ingest; kernel state is CRUD and is visible on the next\n     read. The lock provides atomicity; it cannot provide visibility through\n     an async pipe.\n   - There is no s2s read path back out of the record plane for an ordinary\n     service today: the plane's s2s half is authorized to the record store\n     itself and to nobody else (the superseded shared `query` trait; it named\n     the since-deleted `argus` until 2026-09-01, and phase 2 item 10 cannot\n     delete the trait until the per-service own-record client exists).\n   - An s2s publish is stamped with the PUBLISHING SERVICE's namespace, not\n     the user's (workers/big8-p1-messaging-questions.md §1), so a per-user row\n     written that way is a row no user can read.\n\n What DOES stay a record is the cycle history — AgentCycle below — which is\n append-shaped, does not need read-back by this service, and is what the\n design record's user-facing \"Bird's Eye View\" reads through the generated\n c2s record query plane.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "user",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "user is the principal this cursor belongs to and the collection key.\n\n For a message-triggered cycle it is messaging.Message.recipient — the\n ADDRESSEE, not the sender: the loop that wakes is the recipient's agent.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "cycles_completed",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "cycles_completed counts the loop cycles that have finished for this user.\n Monotonic, and the source of AgentCycle.cycle below.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_fencing_token",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "last_fencing_token is THE FENCE, and it is what makes the lock a fencing\n lock rather than a mutex.\n\n A fencing token is kernel's Raft log index of the granting Acquire:\n strictly increasing over every grant of every key, never reused. A resource\n owner stores the highest token it has seen and REJECTS a write carrying a\n lower one — that, not the lock, is what makes a paused or partitioned stale\n holder safe. This field is that stored highest-seen value, and this service\n is the resource owner doing the comparing (see service.rs `write_cursor`).\n\n Without it, a holder that stalled long enough for its lease to lapse could\n wake up and overwrite the cursor a newer holder had already advanced. The\n lock alone narrows that window; it does not close it.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_cycle_at_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "last_cycle_at_ms is when the last completed cycle finished.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_instance_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "last_instance_id is the instance that ran it. Recorded because consecutive\n cycles for one user legitimately run on different instances and \"which pod\n was that\" is otherwise unanswerable after the fact — NOT because anything\n routes on it. Nothing reads this field to make a decision.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_wake",
            "type": "message",
            "description": "last_wake is the last event that woke a cycle for this user.",
            "message": {
              "name": "WakeEvent",
              "package": "scottyagent",
              "description": "WakeEvent is ONE thing that woke the loop. A cycle carries every event it\n aggregated, not just the first, so \"why did you wake\" has a complete answer\n even when several arrived at once.",
              "fields": [
                {
                  "name": "reason",
                  "type": "enum",
                  "description": "reason is the trigger kind.",
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_enum_defined",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "bool",
                      "bool_value": true,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ],
                  "enum_values": [
                    {
                      "name": "WAKE_REASON_MESSAGE",
                      "number": 1,
                      "description": "A messaging.Message arrived on this user's EventBus queue and this\n instance's consumer group took it."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "source_id",
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "source_id identifies the specific event. For WAKE_REASON_MESSAGE it is the\n messaging.Message's delivery_id — the record key of the one delivery this\n wake is for, not the message_id it shares with its siblings, because the\n delivery is what this consumer group consumed.",
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 512 bytes"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 512,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "observed_at_ms",
                  "type": "int64",
                  "description": "observed_at_ms is when this instance took the event off the queue.",
                  "constraints": [
                    "must be \u003e= 0"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_ge",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 0,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "trace_id",
                  "type": "string",
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "default_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 32768,
                      "defaulted": true,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "default_bound"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "noop",
                  "type": "bool"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "name": "namespace",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "namespace is the row's owning TENANT, by exact string equality against the\n reader's namespace claim.\n\n (spec.store_namespace) here generates the READ-side egress filter on every\n surface that returns this row — including the MCP dispatch, which resolves\n the caller's claim itself, holds each returned row to it, and ABORTS THE\n PROCESS on a row it would otherwise have to drop. That abort is the\n designed behaviour for a genuine tenant violation, not a bug.\n\n WHERE THE VALUE COMES FROM, stated because it is the weak link and hiding\n it would be worse. An event-driven cycle has NO CALLER: nothing\n authenticated this work, so there is no verified claim to stamp from. The\n only tenant signal available is the consumed messaging.Message's own\n `namespace`, and this service copies that. That value is itself stamped by\n eventbus from MESSAGING'S service token rather than from the sender's\n (workers/big8-p1-messaging-questions.md §1, a fleet-wide open gap), so it\n is the right FIELD carrying a value whose provenance is not yet what the\n design intends. Copying it is still correct: it is the only tenant fact the\n event carries, and the moment tacplus grows a delegated s2s publish the\n value becomes the sender's with no change here.\n\n Not a query field: the field exists to BE the egress boundary, and a\n boundary a caller can predicate on is not one.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "trace_id",
            "type": "string",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "default_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "noop",
            "type": "bool"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "trace_id",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
