            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 128,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "filter_mode": "eq"
          },
          {
            "name": "occurred_at_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "occurred_at_ms is the RANGE criterion — \"what happened between D1 and D2\"\n is the auditor's own question.",
            "filter_mode": "range"
          },
          {
            "name": "acted_by",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "acted_by is the principal on whose behalf the write happened. NOT redacted:\n §4 says a finding is a citation naming \"action A by principal X at T\", and\n a citation whose principal egress clears is not a citation.\n It is also the ROW'S OWNING PRINCIPAL for the record query plane's c2s\n half. spiffe_id below is the record's declared owner but is the PUBLISHER\n — eventbus stamps scotty's own service identity onto it — so filtering a\n user's reads on that field would show every user nothing. acted_by is the\n authenticated human the write happened for, taken from the verified token\n (service.rs `principal()`) or from the written row's own (spec.principal)\n field, never from anything the caller writes. That is the value the\n generated wrapper's row filter must compare against.\n The EQ criterion is what carries the row filter into the plane's SIZE-ONLY\n RPCs: Get\u003cT\u003eCount returns a number over the handle's predicate and no rows,\n so no egress filter can narrow it. The generated c2s wrapper pins this\n criterion to the caller at Create\u003cT\u003eQuery, and every later call on the\n handle inherits it.",
