      "name": "min_group_size",
      "type": "int32",
      "description": "min_group_size is the smallest result size this record's size-only query\n surfaces will disclose. 0 / unset = no guard (additive, like owner_default).\n\n \"Size-only\" is the line: it covers the surfaces that return a size and no\n rows (Get\u003cT\u003eCount, Subscribe\u003cT\u003eCount, and the QueryStats.size that\n Update\u003cT\u003eQuery returns for its newly-installed predicate). It deliberately\n does NOT cover List\u003cT\u003e's total_count, which accompanies rows the caller has\n just been handed and so discloses nothing the rows themselves do not. A\n future change that makes a paged read return counts without rows has\n crossed that line."
    },
    {
      "name": "residency",
      "type": "message",
      "description": "residency is how long this record type's rows stay resident in MEMORY.\n\n It is a data-lifecycle property of the type inside ONE store, not a choice\n of backing store: field 50024 (cold_store) was retired above because\n \"tiering is internal to the store service\", and that ruling stands. There is\n still exactly one record store. This names the WINDOW, and hot/warm/cold are\n presets of it.\n\n Unset = RESIDENCY_HOT, which is what every record does today. Additive, the\n same way min_group_size and owner_default are.\n\n Residency is NOT retention. Retention (field 5) DELETES rows and measures\n from the author's business timestamp; residency EVICTS rows from memory and\n measures from the store epoch (the (spec.store_epoch) field, stamped by the\n wrapper on every write). Eviction is cache maintenance: a warm row evicted\n from memory is still complete and queryable on disk.\n\n Example:\n   option (spec.record) = {\n     key: \"accounting_id\" owner: \"spiffe_id\"\n     residency: {window: RESIDENCY_WARM}\n   };",
      "message": {
        "name": "ResidencyPolicy",
        "package": "spec",
        "description": "ResidencyPolicy states a record type's memory-residency window.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "window",
            "type": "enum",
            "description": "window is the preset. Required when residency is declared at all;\n RESIDENCY_UNSPECIFIED inside a present ResidencyPolicy is a generation\n error, not a silent HOT (an author who wrote the block meant something).",
            "enum_values": [
              {
                "name": "RESIDENCY_HOT",
                "number": 1,
                "description": "HOT: in memory forever, or until deleted. Window = unbounded."
              },
              {
                "name": "RESIDENCY_WARM",
                "number": 2,
                "description": "WARM: in memory for a window; complete and queryable on disk beneath it.\n Window = ResidencyPolicy.window_seconds, or the configured default when\n that is 0. Rows are evicted from memory at window age and served from disk\n thereafter; a write returns the row to the window."
              },
              {
                "name": "RESIDENCY_COLD",
                "number": 3,
                "description": "COLD: RESERVED, NOT IMPLEMENTED. Semantics would be window = 0 — straight\n to disk, never resident — if a high-volume need ever appears. Declaring it\n FAILS GENERATION (see ValidateResidencyAnnotations). The value exists so the\n refusal can be explicit instead of the declaration being a typo nobody\n catches."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "window_seconds",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "window_seconds overrides the configured default window. WARM ONLY:\n set on HOT or COLD it is a knob nothing reads, and generation REJECTS it\n rather than accept an intent tacplus does not honour.\n 0 = use the configured default."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "mutability",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "mutability is whether this record type's rows may be changed after they are\n written. It is an ATTRIBUTE, orthogonal to residency: immutable-warm\n (accounting) and mutable-warm are both legitimate, and so is either with\n RESIDENCY_HOT.\n\n Unset = MUTABILITY_CRUD, which is what every record does today.\n\n MUTABILITY_APPEND_ONLY is enforced at BOTH ends or it is decoration: the\n generator emits no row-mutating RPC for the type (ValidateMutabilityAnnotations\n refuses one at generation), and the generated service wrapper refuses to host\n one. This is the same two-ended shape (spec.record).min_group_size already has.",
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "MUTABILITY_CRUD",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "CRUD: rows may be updated and deleted by key."
        },
        {
          "name": "MUTABILITY_APPEND_ONLY",
          "number": 2,
          "description": "APPEND_ONLY: rows are written once. No row-mutating RPC is generated for\n the type and the generated wrapper refuses to host one. Its disk slices are\n pure appends — sealed, checksummable units."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "s2s_subscribe",
      "type": "message (recursive reference: spec.MethodS2SAnnotation)",
      "description": "s2s_subscribe, when present, exposes the record's SUBSCRIBE RPC to the named\n internal services. It is the consumer half of the pair whose producer half\n is s2s (field 4), and it exists because a topic has two boundaries and only\n one of them was declarable.\n\n A record's synthesized \u003cRecord\u003eBus topic wrapper hosts both PublishTo\u003cT\u003e and\n SubscribeTo\u003cT\u003e. s2s names who may PUBLISH. Nothing named who may CONSUME, so\n the subscribe RPC could only ever fall back to the state service's\n service-level list, which for a synthesized wrapper is resolved from the\n owning PACKAGE — and the record store that consumes every record topic is\n never in the owning package. Under per-topic authorization that is\n fail-closed: the store is denied on every topic it exists to read.\n\n Declared rather than inferred on purpose. Treating \"the record store\" as an\n implicitly authorized consumer would put an undeclared trust relationship in\n the generator with no proto anyone could read to audit it, and would silently\n grant or silently withhold access to any FUTURE consumer of a record topic.\n Who may read a record's stream is exactly as much a policy statement as who\n may write it, so it is written down in the same place, in the same shape.\n\n Same semantics as s2s: [\"*\"] is any authenticated service, an explicit list\n is exactly that list, and an absent annotation leaves the service-level\n default in place (i.e. this is additive — a record that declares none keeps\n today's behaviour).\n\n Example — a stored record consumed by the record store:\n   option (spec.record) = {\n     key: \"accounting_id\" owner: \"spiffe_id\"\n     s2s: {auth_services: [\"*\"]}              // anyone may publish\n     s2s_subscribe: {auth_services: [\"stratum\"]}  // only the store may read\n   };"
    },
    {
      "name": "s2s_read",
      "type": "message (recursive reference: spec.MethodS2SAnnotation)",
      "description": "s2s_read, when present, exposes this record's STORED-ROW READ surface to the\n named internal services — the record query plane's S2S half, hosted by the\n record store.\n\n It is the third member of a set whose other two already exist, and it is\n declared for exactly the reason s2s_subscribe is. A record type has three\n boundaries, not two:\n\n   s2s            who may PUBLISH onto the record's topic  (write the stream)\n   s2s_subscribe  who may CONSUME the record's topic       (tail the stream)\n   s2s_read       who may READ the record's STORED ROWS    (query the store)\n\n Until this field existed the third boundary had no per-record declaration at\n all: every record's read RPCs carried the TRAIT-WIDE (spec.trait).auth_services\n list, so authorizing one service to read one record type authorized it to read\n EVERY record type in the fleet. That is precisely what\n decisions/mcp-generation-and-service-owned-data-2026-08-31.md §2 rules out —\n \"Stratum grants entitlements per service, per record type, INDEPENDENTLY\" —\n and it is why that document's §1 calls the shared `query` trait superseded.\n The trait-wide list stays as the fallback for records that declare nothing,\n so this is additive exactly the way s2s_subscribe, min_group_size and\n owner_default are.\n\n Declared rather than inferred, on the same reasoning s2s_subscribe records:\n treating \"the service that produces a record type\" as an implicitly\n authorized reader would put an undeclared trust relationship in the\n generator with no proto anyone could read to audit it. A producer is not\n automatically a reader — §2's whole point is that read and write rights are\n granted independently — so the read right is written down where the other two\n are, in the same shape.\n\n PAIRED CONTROL. This is the grant half. The USE half is\n (spec.record_read) on the reading service's own proto, and the generator\n refuses a build where one is declared without the other: a service that\n declares (spec.record_read) on a record whose s2s_read does not name it\n fails generation rather than emitting a client that would be denied at\n runtime. Neither end can drift from the other, because neither end works\n alone.\n\n Same semantics as s2s and s2s_subscribe: [\"*\"] is any authenticated service,\n an explicit list is exactly that list, and an absent annotation leaves the\n trait-wide default in place.\n\n Declaring it also makes the record's KEY field an EQ criterion on the\n generated \u003cT\u003eFilter, whether or not the key carries its own\n (spec.query_field). Point-read-by-key is the read surface's most basic\n question and the plane could not express it otherwise; see\n record_map.go's keyFilterCriterion.\n\n Example — a record its own producer reads back:\n   option (spec.record) = {\n     key: \"delivery_id\" owner: \"spiffe_id\"\n     s2s: {auth_services: [\"messaging\"]}\n     s2s_subscribe: {auth_services: [\"stratum\"]}\n     s2s_read: {auth_services: [\"messaging\"]}\n   };"
    },
    {
      "name": "c2s_read",
      "type": "message (recursive reference: spec.MethodS2SAnnotation)",
      "description": "c2s_read, when present, names the services allowed to RE-EXPOSE this\n record's stored rows TO THEIR OWN END USERS — the client-facing read plane,\n hosted by the reading service itself and never by the record store.\n\n It is the FOURTH boundary, and it is not a narrowing of the third:\n\n   s2s            who may PUBLISH onto the record's topic  (write the stream)\n   s2s_subscribe  who may CONSUME the record's topic       (tail the stream)\n   s2s_read       who may READ the record's STORED ROWS    (query the store)\n   c2s_read       who may DISCLOSE those rows TO A PERSON  (re-expose them)\n\n A service can hold s2s_read without c2s_read and that combination is the\n common one: read a record type to make an internal decision, and never show\n it to anyone. Making disclosure a separate grant is what keeps that\n distinction sayable, and it keeps the RECORD OWNER in the loop on every\n re-exposure of its data rather than only on the first read of it.\n\n TWO SEMANTIC DIFFERENCES FROM s2s_read, both deliberate:\n\n   [\"*\"] FAILS GENERATION. On s2s_read the wildcard means \"any authenticated\n       peer inside the fleet\", which is broad but is a statement someone\n       might mean. \"Any service may show my rows to any of its users\" is not\n       a statement anyone means to make, so it is refused rather than\n       supported.\n   c2s_read IMPLIES s2s_read for the same service, and the pair with the\n       second half missing fails generation. The generated c2s handler's\n       entire data path IS the s2s reader; a disclosure grant without a read\n       grant is a surface that cannot execute.\n\n PAIRED CONTROL, exactly as s2s_read is. This is the grant half; the use half\n is (spec.record_read).c2s on the reading service's own proto, and\n ValidateRecordReadAnnotations refuses a build in which one is declared\n without the other, in both directions.\n\n Example — a record its producer also shows to its users:\n   option (spec.record) = {\n     key: \"activity_id\" owner: \"spiffe_id\"\n     s2s:           {auth_services: [\"scotty\"]}\n     s2s_subscribe: {auth_services: [\"stratum\"]}\n     s2s_read:      {auth_services: [\"scotty\"]}\n     c2s_read:      {auth_services: [\"scotty\"]}\n   };"
    }
  ]
}

fields.description:
retention is the store-neutral row retention policy for this record's
 stored rows (unset = indefinite).

 Retention is DELIVERY-side state, not transformation state. A
 (spec.transformation) is a pure typed conversion A -> B; a pure function has
 no lifetime, so a retention window cannot live on it. It lives here, on the
 declaration that says this message is stored and delivered — the same place
 key/owner/producer-surface live. A downstream materializer (e.g. stratum's
 EventStoreGenerator) reads it from here to render the concrete eviction rule.

 Example:
   option (spec.record) = {
     key: "event_id" owner: "spiffe_id" s2s: {auth_services: ["*"]}
     retention: {timestamp_field: "timestamp_ms" duration_seconds: 7776000}
   };

window is the preset. Required when residency is declared at all;
 RESIDENCY_UNSPECIFIED inside a present ResidencyPolicy is a generation
 error, not a silent HOT (an author who wrote the block meant something).

types.schema_json:
{
  "name": "RecordRead",
  "package": "spec",
  "description": "RecordRead declares that this service reads a (spec.record) type's STORED\n ROWS through the record query plane's S2S half.\n\n The USE half of the paired control whose grant half is\n (spec.record).s2s_read. See spec.record_read in the FileOptions block above\n for what it generates and why the transport dependency is implied rather than\n restated.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "record",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "record is the fully-qualified record message name, \"package.Message\"\n (e.g. \"messaging.Message\"). Proto package names are globally unique, so no\n file path is needed; tacplus resolves the name to its declaring file.\n\n A name that resolves to no message, or to a message carrying no\n (spec.record), is a hard generation failure — the same fail-loud posture\n (spec.record).key already takes for a key naming a field that does not\n exist.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "c2s",
      "type": "message",
      "description": "c2s, when present, asks for a CLIENT-FACING read surface over this record's\n rows, emitted into THIS service's own proto as a synthesized sibling\n service (`\u003cService\u003eRecords`) carrying Get\u003cT\u003e and List\u003cT\u003e.\n\n Absent — which is every declaration that predates this field — the reader\n gets the s2s typed reader and nothing else, byte for byte as before.\n\n The grant half is (spec.record).c2s_read on the record, and it must name\n this service. See the C2SScope comments for what the emitted surface is\n allowed to return.",
      "message": {
        "name": "RecordReadC2S",
        "package": "spec",
        "description": "RecordReadC2S is the USE half of the client-facing read plane: what\n (spec.record_read).c2s asks tacplus to emit.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "scope",
            "type": "enum",
            "description": "scope names which rows the surface may return. See C2SScope.",
            "enum_values": [
              {
                "name": "C2S_SCOPE_PRINCIPAL",
                "number": 1,
                "description": "PRINCIPAL: rows whose owning-principal field equals the caller's subject,\n AND whose namespace equals the caller's tenant. \"My own rows.\"\n\n The principal field is resolved exactly as the egress row filter resolves\n it — (spec.store_principal) on a record field, else (spec.record).owner —\n and ONLY when that field is also an EQ criterion on the generated \u003cT\u003eFilter.\n When it resolves to nothing, generation FAILS rather than degrading to a\n tenant-wide read: on ScottyActivity and AccountingRecord the declared\n `owner` is the PUBLISHING SERVICE, so a filter on it would show a human\n every row in its tenant while looking like it showed them their own."
              },
              {
                "name": "C2S_SCOPE_NAMESPACE",
                "number": 2,
                "description": "NAMESPACE: every row in the caller's tenant, with no principal narrowing.\n A legitimate shape — an org-wide audit feed — but a disclosure decision, so\n it must be written down and can never be what absence means.\n\n DECLARED BUT NOT IMPLEMENTED in v1: generation fails with an explicit\n \"declared but not implemented\" error, the same stance RESIDENCY_COLD takes.\n The value exists so it cannot be invented twice with two meanings."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "exposure",
            "type": "enum",
            "description": "exposure is stamped onto the emitted RPCs' (spec.rpc). INTERNAL (the\n default) keeps the surface inside the cluster boundary; EXTERNAL publishes\n it through the gateway, which is what a mobile or browser client needs.",
            "enum_values": [
              {
                "name": "RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL",
                "number": 0,
                "description": "RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL: reachable only from inside the cluster boundary."
              },
              {
                "name": "RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL",
                "number": 1,
                "description": "RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL: reachable from outside the cluster boundary."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}

